# Load detailed instructions only for enabled tool families.
import "tool_catalog"

define tool_manual with family returning Str:
    if family == "system":
        return "Use the exact verified paths in HOST ENVIRONMENT.system_commands, never a bare executable name. To open a document in a named macOS application use the open executable with argv [open_path, \"-a\", application_name, absolute_file_path]. A zero exit proves the OS accepted the launch request, not the application window contents. Use process_run for host commands: argv is a literal array with an absolute executable path, cwd is an absolute working directory, and timeout_seconds is 1-60. No shell expansion occurs unless you explicitly invoke a shell. Commands can have external effects. Use process_retry to deliberately rerun a finished command after repairing or changing its inputs: provide operation_id from the original output_ref and a reason. The EP runtime retrieves the exact original argv, cwd and timeout; do not reconstruct or change those arguments. Identical process_run calls replay their saved receipt, while one process_retry intent can execute once. Repeating the same operation_id retry replays its child receipt. Use process_output to inspect old output and original_command when needed. An uncertain, timed-out or cancelled operation cannot use this retry path. Report the actual exit code and termination; stderr alone is not failure. Do not rerun a command to obtain more output. Use process_output with query OPERATION_ID:stdout:BYTE_OFFSET or OPERATION_ID:stderr:BYTE_OFFSET, taking OPERATION_ID from output_ref and continuing with next_offset. Prefer the exact stdout_read_query or stderr_read_query in the receipt; for the final portion use stdout_tail_query or stderr_tail_query. Saved output is in the journal, so do not search directories for it. A replayed receipt means no new execution occurred. An uncertain receipt requires inspecting recorded effects, not blind retry. Use execution as the answer basis for successful command execution."
    if family == "documents":
        return "Use docx_read for Word .docx documents: supply the absolute path and character offset, starting at zero. Continue with next_offset and cite the returned file URL. Its scope covers main body text and tables, not layout or images. Use pdf_read for PDFs: supply the absolute path, one-based page and character offset (start at page 1, offset 0). Continue with next_offset or another page, and cite the returned file URL and page. An image-only page can have no extractable text; do not invent its contents. For local files, file_list takes an absolute path and an after cursor: begin with an empty cursor and use next_after for subsequent pages. Listings prove names only, not file contents."
    if family == "memory":
        return "Use memory_assess to record a claim assessment without changing its original text: take note_id and belief_revision from beliefs[] or BELIEF METADATA, send revision=belief_revision, state tentative/supported/disputed/superseded/expired, valid_from and valid_until as real UTC YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ or empty when unknown, evidence_id as a retained independent user/source/calculation receipt ID or empty, and reason. Never invent dates or evidence. Supported means attributed retained evidence, not certain truth; user testimony stays attributed. Expired/future claims are excluded from current recall but remain inspectable through memory_open and history. When only certainty, dispute or validity changes, use memory_assess and preserve the original note. Use memory_correct only to change the wording of an active semantic or procedural note when the user corrects it: provide note_id, corrected text, and reason. Take note_id from notes[].note_id in a memory-search/open result, or the first column of the retrieved note row. The top-level memory_id is a tool receipt ID, never the note to correct. Use memory_search or memory_open to find the right note; never invent an ID. This keeps the old note historical and makes the replacement active. Use memory_note for a new fact without a prior note."
    if family == "tasks":
        return "For a commitment, to-do, deferred job or request to create a task, use task_create with objective and an observable success_condition. A memory_note is not a tracked task. task_create records a planned task; do not execute it when the user asks to defer it. After a successful task_create receipt, confirm the saved task using experience as the answer basis; no extra lookup is needed just to confirm creation. Task receipts include tasks with explicit task_id, revision, status, objective and success_condition. These are authoritative saved checkpoints. The records rows also contain task_id first and revision second. Active task checkpoints in working state contain task_id, revision, objective, success_condition and next_action. Resume those existing tasks instead of creating duplicates. Use task_open only when you need the latest checkpoint; query must be the bare numeric task_id string, for example 4, without a task_id: prefix. Use task_update with that task_id and current revision to record progress. Completion requires evidence_id from the successful work tool receipt memory_id, never from task_create, task_open, notes or your own prose. Failed tools cannot complete a task: record failed or waiting with its reason instead. Only mark tasks completed with actual evidence."
    return ""
